<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brahma is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god of creation, who with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> forms the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trimurti">Trimurti</a>. He is usually shown with four faces and four arms, seated on a lotus that rises from the navel of Vishnu. Although he features prominently in myth, he has relatively few temples, and dynasties such as the Pallavas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> claimed descent from him through epic heroes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brahma.jpg" alt="Brahma" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brahma,_dal_soffitto_del_tempio_di_huchchappaiyya_gudi,_aihole_(balgakot),_karnataka,_VII_secolo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of the term brahmā are uncertain, partly because several related words occur in Vedic literature, such as Brahman for the Ultimate Reality and Brāhmaṇa for priest. Brahman is a genderless, abstract metaphysical concept, while Brahmā is a masculine god, and some scholars suggest the god may have emerged as a visible icon of the impersonal principle. A distinct god named Brahma is evidenced in late Vedic texts, and he is frequently identified with Prajapati. One of the earliest mentions of Brahma with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> is in the Maitrayaniya Upanishad, probably composed around the late 1st millennium BCE, which links Brahma to the quality called rajas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the Puranas Brahma is a secondary creator who creates all forms in the universe but not the primordial universe itself, and his activity depends on a higher god or goddess. Vishnu-focused Puranas say he was born from a lotus emerging from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>'s navel. In the Brahmanda Purana there was only an eternal ocean, from which a golden egg called Hiranyagarbha emerged, and Brahma came into being within it. He creates but neither preserves nor destroys, and like all deities he is mortal. His wife is the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a>.</p><p>Brahma is traditionally depicted with four faces, each facing a cardinal direction and credited with creating one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, and four arms holding symbols of knowledge and creation. He often has a white beard and sits on a lotus with a swan, the hamsa, as his mount.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the post-Vedic period Brahma was a prominent god, and a sect of him existed from the 2nd to the 6th century CE. By the 7th century he had lost importance. Historians point to the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> and attacks by Buddhists, Jains and followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Today he does not enjoy popular worship, and few temples are dedicated to him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the most prominent being the Brahma Temple at Pushkar. His shrines are also found outside India: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and at the Erawan Shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> derives from Brahma.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brahma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal religion was Hindu, the brick sanctuaries of Isanapura centred on lingas, with Harihara, <b>Brahma</b>, Indra and Lakshmi also worshipped and Buddhism tolerated beside them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pallava kings ruled as Hindu monarchs in the Sanskrit tradition, claiming descent from the god <b>Brahma</b> through the epic hero Ashvatthama and legitimating their power through Vedic sacrifices, temple building and grants to Brahmins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kalachuris themselves were weakened by Bijjala’s assassination c. 1167 and by unrest among their vassals, and in 1184 Tailapa III’s son Someshvara IV, aided by the general <b>Brahma</b>, restored the Chalukya dynasty at Kalyani.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When Sogdian Buddhists translated their scriptures, they rendered the Indian gods <b>Brahma</b>, Indra and Shiva by the names of Sogdian gods: Zurvan, Adbag and Weshparkar.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The compound is dedicated to the Trimūrti, the expression of God as Creator (<b>Brahma</b>), Preserver (Vishnu) and Destroyer (Shiva).</blockquote><a href="/work/prambanan">Prambanan · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>During Kūkai's administration of the Sōgō, further ceremonies were added, including the Bodhisattva Precepts from the <b>Brahma</b> Net Sutra and the esoteric precepts of Kūkai's new Shingon school, and an Abhiseka Hall for initiating monks of the six Nara schools was in place by 829.</blockquote><a href="/work/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
